<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahi Kantha Agency</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as British Mahi Kantha Agency</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahi Kantha Agency was a British political agency in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> that supervised the many small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and Koli chiefships of the Mahi Kantha, the country around the Mahi and Sabarmati rivers. Most of its states paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> of Baroda. After independence they were merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> and are now in Gujarat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahi-kantha-agency.jpg" alt="Mahi Kantha Agency" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en, following request by Nizil Shah · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahi_Kantha_Agency_in_Gujarat_during_British_India_1820-1933.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
